
  [1mError handling[0m — three cases, deliberately different:

  - [3mValidation failures[0m are not exceptional. They're data in [38;5;153merrors[39m, rendered to the page. First failing rule per field
    wins, so a user sees one message per field, not a pile.
  - [3mSchema/markup mismatch[0m — a schema names a field with no matching [38;5;153m[name][39m input. That's a developer bug and silently
    passing an [38;5;153mundefined[39m value would hide it, so [38;5;153mbindForm[39m throws on bind. A missing [38;5;153m[data-error-for][39m span is not fatal:
    the message is skipped rather than throwing, since a form may intentionally not display some errors.
  - [3m[38;5;153monValid[39m throwing[0m is the form's problem, not the binder's — it propagates untouched. Swallowing it would hide real
    login failures.

  Empty/missing values normalize to [38;5;153m""[39m before validators run, so [38;5;153mrequired()[39m never has to defend against [38;5;153mundefined[39m.

  [1mTesting[0m — Node's built-in runner ([38;5;153mnode --test[39m), no dependencies, [38;5;153mnpm test[39m wired up in [38;5;153mpackage.json[39m:

  - [38;5;153mvalidation.test.js[39m covers the pure core directly: each validator's pass and fail case, [38;5;153mvalidate[39m with multi-rule
    fields, first-error-wins ordering, cross-field access via [38;5;153mallValues[39m, and the all-valid case.
  - The binder gets thinner coverage — it needs a DOM, and you turned down E2E. I'll test the two pieces that are pure
    functions (value collection and error rendering) by exporting them and driving them with a hand-built fake element,
    and leave the actual submit-event wiring to manual verification in the browser. I'd rather say that plainly than
    claim the binder is covered.
  - Manual check after implementation: serve the directory, confirm empty-field messages appear per field and a filled
    form still logs the login result.

  [1mNot doing[0m (YAGNI, no second form to justify them): live/on-blur validation, async validators, i18n of messages, a
  [38;5;153mvalidateField[39m public entry point, and anything touching [38;5;153msrc/[39m.
